Provide an example of how a table of contents can be generated automatically in a LATEX document. Make sure your example contains the necessary commands.

[Response A]
Here is an example of how to generate a table of contents (TOC) for a LaTeX document using the `toc` package:

```
\documentclass{article}
% Add toc command here if needed

\tocset{
  \listofcontents,
  \tableofcontents,
}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section*{\Large Table of Contents}
\printindex
\end{document}
```

In this example, we first set up our TOC by adding `\listofcontents`, which lists all sections and subsections that will appear on the page. We also add `\tableofcontents`, which generates the actual list of tables.

Next, we use the `\printindex` command to print out any index entries or subheadings within the current section. Finally, we call `\maketitle` at the end of the document to ensure it includes the title of the chapter/section being discussed.

Note: The exact syntax may vary depending on whether you're generating a PDF version of the document or including it as part of a larger project such as a book or thesis. However, most modern TeX distributions come with built-in support for automatic generation of TOCs.

